use std
into spectrum.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sp_ is exported). It runs a response spectrum analysis on the vibration modes of a voxel finite element model: it reads a base acceleration spectrum (a table, the ASCE 7 design spectrum or a constant), works out each mode's participation in the shaking direction, its peak modal displacement and base force, and combines the modes by SRSS or CQC into peak nodal displacements, element stresses (von Mises from the combined stress components, averaged to the nodes) and the base shear. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn sp_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sp_view_f64";
extern fn sp_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "sp_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NM` (modes), `NN` (grid nodes), `NP` (spectrum table points), `GX`, `GY` and `GZ` (elements along x, y and z). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (64 inputs), `OUT` (64 outputs), `OM` (NM circular frequencies in rad/s), `PH` (NM mode shapes one after another, 3 NN values each: x, y and z at node n at 3 n, 3 n plus 1 and 3 n plus 2), `MV` (3 NN lumped masses, one per degree of freedom, 0.0 where the node is held), `SF` (NP spectrum frequencies in Hz, rising), `SA` (NP spectrum accelerations in g), `MT` (8 per mode, the mode table), `DB` (144: the 6 by 24 matrix, row by row, that turns the 24 node displacements of one element into its 6 center stresses), `U` (3 NN combined peak displacements), `UN` (NN combined displacement magnitudes), `VM` (NN nodal von Mises stresses), `CN` (NN counts), `RH` (NM times NM modal correlations), `CF` (NM modal displacement scale: the modal displacement times the mass-normalizing factor), `Q` (6 NM scratch), `UE` (24 scratch) and `CV` (curve, 2 per point). Growable i32 array `SOL` (GX times GY times GZ solid flags, element e at (k times GY plus j) times GX plus i).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again. `at1(n)` returns n, or 1 when n is below 1.

The exported function `sp_views()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, registers the arrays with `sp_view_f64`: 1 IN, 2 OUT, 3 OM, 4 PH, 5 MV, 6 SF, 7 SA, 8 MT, 9 DB, 10 U, 11 UN, 12 VM, 13 RH, 14 CV; and with `sp_view_i32`: 20 SOL. It returns 0.

The exported function `sp_init()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, sizes IN to 64 zeros and OUT to 64 zeros with `zf`, calls `sp_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `sp_size(nm, nn, np, gx, gy, gz)`, declared with the export keyword with six i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NM, NN, NP, GX, GY and GZ, then sizes with `zf` to zeros, each size raised to at least 1 with `at1`: OM to nm, PH to nm times 3 nn, MV to 3 nn, SF and SA to np, MT to 8 nm, DB to 144, U to 3 nn, UN to nn, VM to nn, CN to nn, RH to nm times nm, CF to nm, Q to 6 nm, UE to 24 and CV to 2; and with `zi` SOL to gx times gy times gz zeros. It calls `sp_views()` and returns 0.

Spectrum helper. `sa(f)` takes a frequency f in Hz and returns the spectral acceleration in g. With kind IN[0] as int: kind 2 returns IN[4]. Kind 1 is the ASCE 7 design spectrum with SDS IN[1], SD1 IN[2] and TL IN[3]: T is 1.0 over f when f is above 1e-9 and 1e9 otherwise; T0 is 0.2 times SD1 over SDS and TS is SD1 over SDS (both 0.0 when SDS is not above 0.0, and then it returns 0.0); it returns SDS times (0.4 plus 0.6 times T over T0) when T is below T0, SDS when T is at most TS, SD1 over T when T is at most TL, and SD1 times TL over T squared otherwise. Any other kind reads the table: with NP below 1 it returns 0.0; when f is at most SF[0] it returns SA[0]; when f is at least SF[NP minus 1] it returns SA[NP minus 1]; otherwise i is the last index below NP minus 1 with SF[i] at most f, and t is the fraction of the way f lies from SF[i] to SF[i plus 1]: log(f over SF[i]) over log(SF[i plus 1] over SF[i]) when f, SF[i] and SF[i plus 1] are all above 0.0 (and SF[i plus 1] differs from SF[i]), else (f minus SF[i]) over (SF[i plus 1] minus SF[i]) or 0.0 when they are equal; when SA[i] and SA[i plus 1] are both above 0.0 it returns exp(log(SA[i]) plus t times (log(SA[i plus 1]) minus log(SA[i]))), else SA[i] plus t times (SA[i plus 1] minus SA[i]).

The exported function `sp_curve(n)`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 parameter (converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sizes CV to 2 times n zeros (at least 2) with `zf` and calls `sp_views()`. With f0 IN[10] and f1 IN[11] (f0 raised to at least 1e-3 and f1 to at least f0 times 1.001), point i below n has frequency f0 times (f1 over f0) to the power i over (n minus 1) as reals (just f0 when n is 1), stored at CV[2 i], and sa of that frequency at CV[2 i plus 1]. Returns n.

Correlation helper. `rho(wi, wj, z)` returns the CQC correlation of two modes with circular frequencies wi and wj and damping ratio z: 1.0 when wi or wj is not above 0.0 and they are equal, 0.0 when either is not above 0.0 otherwise; else with r equal to wj over wi it is 8.0 times z squared times (1.0 plus r) times r to the power 1.5, divided by ((1.0 minus r squared) squared plus 4.0 times z squared times r times (1.0 plus r) squared).

Combination helper. `comb(a, m)` takes an f64 array a by reference holding m modal values at a[0] to a[m minus 1] and returns the square root of the larger of 0.0 and the double sum over i and j below m of RH[i times NM plus j] times a[i] times a[j].

The exported function `sp_run()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, does the analysis and returns the number of modes with a circular frequency above 1e-9. Inputs: IN[5] the shaking direction d as int (0 x, 1 y, 2 z), IN[6] the damping ratio z (raised to at least 1e-4), IN[7] the combination (below 0.5 SRSS, otherwise CQC), IN[8] the acceleration of gravity in model units and IN[9] the response scale (Ie over R; 1.0 when it is not above 0.0).

Step 1, modes. mf is the sum of MV[3 n plus d] over nodes n below NN. For each mode j below NM, with b equal to j times 3 NN: s is the sum over k below 3 NN of MV[k] times PH[b plus k] squared, and sc is 1.0 over its square root (0.0 when s is not above 0.0); gam is sc times the sum over n below NN of MV[3 n plus d] times PH[b plus 3 n plus d]; w is OM[j] and f is w over 2 pi; a is sa(f), and acc is a times IN[8] times the response scale; dj is gam times acc over w squared when w is above 1e-9 and 0.0 otherwise; force is gam squared times acc when w is above 1e-9 and 0.0 otherwise; the running sum cum goes up by gam squared over mf when w is above 1e-9 (0.0 when mf is not above 0.0). CF[j] becomes dj times sc. The row MT[8 j] to MT[8 j plus 7] gets f, gam, gam squared over mf (0.0 when mf is not above 0.0), cum, a, dj, force and sc. OUT[5] becomes mf and OUT[3] the final cum.

Step 2, correlations. RH[i times NM plus j] for i and j below NM is 1.0 when i equals j and 0.0 otherwise when IN[7] is below 0.5, else rho(OM[i], OM[j], z). OUT[2], the base shear, is comb of the modal forces MT[8 j plus 6] (copied into Q at index j for each mode j).

Step 3, displacements. For each degree of freedom k below 3 NN, Q[j] becomes CF[j] times PH[j times 3 NN plus k] for each mode j and U[k] becomes comb(Q, NM). Then UN[n] is the square root of U[3 n] squared plus U[3 n plus 1] squared plus U[3 n plus 2] squared for each node n below NN, and OUT[0] is the largest UN.

Step 4, stresses. VM and CN are filled with 0.0 and OUT[4] starts at 0.0. With NX equal to GX plus 1 and NY equal to GY plus 1, the node at grid point (i, j, k) is (k times NY plus j) times NX plus i. For each element (i, j, k) with i below GX, j below GY and k below GZ whose SOL entry is not 0: its 8 nodes in order are the grid points (i, j, k), (i plus 1, j, k), (i plus 1, j plus 1, k), (i, j plus 1, k), (i, j, k plus 1), (i plus 1, j, k plus 1), (i plus 1, j plus 1, k plus 1) and (i, j plus 1, k plus 1). For each mode m below NM: UE[3 a plus c] for node a below 8 and c below 3 is CF[m] times PH[m times 3 NN plus 3 times node a plus c], and Q[6 m plus r] for r below 6 is the sum over c below 24 of DB[24 r plus c] times UE[c]. Then for each r below 6, s_r is the square root of the larger of 0.0 and the double sum over modes i and j of RH[i times NM plus j] times Q[6 i plus r] times Q[6 j plus r]. The element von Mises stress v is the square root of 0.5 times ((s0 minus s1) squared plus (s1 minus s2) squared plus (s2 minus s0) squared) plus 3.0 times (s3 squared plus s4 squared plus s5 squared); OUT[4] keeps the largest v, and each of the 8 nodes gets v added to VM and 1.0 added to CN. Last, VM[n] is divided by CN[n] wherever CN[n] is above 0.0, and OUT[1] is the largest VM.

Returns the count of modes j with OM[j] above 1e-9.

The `main()` function does nothing.
